WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:05.760
Film Courage: Dan, how was it to get your
first job as a script reader and (if you can)

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:08.400
paint a little bit [of a picture] of your
life at that time?

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:15.159
Daniel Calvisi: Well, I’d gotten out of
NYU film school and I definitely was interested

00:00:15.159 --> 00:00:16.159
in screenwriting.

00:00:16.159 --> 00:00:20.980
That was kind of my focus coming out of film
school.

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:25.119
I really wanted to get into script reading
and I had been interning at the Tribeca Film

00:00:25.119 --> 00:00:30.330
Center in New York for a company that was
co-owned by Robert De Niro’s production

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:33.879
company and in the same building was Miramax
Films.&nbsp;

00:00:33.879 --> 00:00:39.030
And so I just kind of got to know some people
there.

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:43.520
And it’s funny, my first reading job for
them was for their genre division called Dimension

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:45.129
films.&nbsp;

00:00:45.129 --> 00:00:56.020
I remember getting a referral but the guy
said that they were not hiring now “Don’t

00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:57.609
bother calling them.”

00:00:57.609 --> 00:01:03.179
And because I had a lot of initiative, I called
anyway.

00:01:03.179 --> 00:01:07.910
The first thing the supervisor said to me
when he picked up the phone was “Oh…we

00:01:07.910 --> 00:01:09.380
have a reader job open.

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:11.140
Do you want to test for it?”

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:16.490
So I did a test coverage which is they give
you a script that they’ve already had coverage

00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:17.950
on.

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:23.951
I did coverage on it and they evaluate you
on that coverage because they already know

00:01:23.951 --> 00:01:25.500
the script really well.

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:28.560
And so that’s how I got working for them.

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:33.829
Then I transferred over to the Miramax division
which was their parent company and I had also

00:01:33.829 --> 00:01:39.060
been freelancing for a number of other companies
in New York City (other production companies

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:40.060
and studios).

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:46.500
Film Courage: When you came to New York to
attend NYU do you remember some of the scripts

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:50.649
in your mind that you thought “This is the
genre I want to write in.

00:01:50.649 --> 00:01:53.030
This is what I want to create”?

00:01:53.030 --> 00:02:04.039
Daniel Calvisi: It’s funny because growing
up I was more into Spielberg’s work or more

00:02:04.039 --> 00:02:07.270
action stuff like RAMBO and that kind of stuff.

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:11.920
Then before I went to NYU (I transferred in
as a junior), I started getting into Scorsese

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:18.410
and I was watching TAXI DRIVER, GOODFELLAS
and those kind of movies…and Oliver Stone.

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:24.290
And I knew that Scorsese and Oliver Stone
and Spike Lee had gone to NYU so I really

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:25.970
wanted to go there.

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:32.510
I applied and I got in just on the waitlist
and then they called really late in the summer

00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:36.860
before my junior year and said “We have
an opening on the waitlist.”

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:39.650
So that’s why I went to NYU.

00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:42.410
That’s how I was able to get in.

00:02:42.410 --> 00:02:48.440
And it was just really cool to be in New York
where these filmmakers that I really admired

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:49.440
had gone.

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:56.140
So Scorsese, Oliver Stone, Spike Lee, The
Coen Brothers, these were all filmmakers that

00:02:56.140 --> 00:02:58.790
I wanted to emulate.

00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:06.200
Film Courage: So going back to what your jobs
or tasks were, you finally get in.

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:08.590
You were told “Sorry…don’t even bother.”

00:03:08.590 --> 00:03:13.360
You give it a shot anyway, it turns out (as
luck would have it), the do have a slot.

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:18.490
Do you remember what your first few weeks
were as this script reader?

00:03:18.490 --> 00:03:24.760
Daniel Calvisi: I remember that I got scripts
and books because I was in New York and that

00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:30.920
was the literary capital, so I would read
a lot of books ahead of publication.

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:39.010
The agents and book editors would send a book
out to the studios to see if any studios or

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:41.900
production companies wanted to option the
movie rights.

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:46.480
And so I would be the first reader at the
production company or studio to read that

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:52.310
unpublished galley version and that was hard
to do because a lot of times they would literally

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:59.010
give you a 600-page book to read overnight
and you had to write up a 3-5 page report

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:03.880
about it by Noon the next day.

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:04.880
That was hard to do.

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:11.760
I actually got let go from one of my jobs
early on because I just couldn’t turn over

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:13.890
the material that fast.

00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:22.480
So I had to learn on the job, trial-by-fire
and just learned to read really fast and to

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:26.000
intuit that movie structure.

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:31.760
As I am reading this book, I’m asking myself
“Will this transfer over to a movie?

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:39.000
Does it have that potential for that 3-4 act
structure that 95% of great movies use.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:41.350
Film Courage: And so it was all about structure?

00:04:41.350 --> 00:04:42.750
There wasn’t also dialogue?

00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:46.320
Daniel Calvisi: No…well it was evaluating
the whole thing.

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:52.150
As a script reader I was evaluating the whole
script, structure, dialogue, character development,

00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:54.690
all that stuff.

00:04:54.690 --> 00:05:01.480
But if you’re reading something like a novel
or a graphic novel or a piece from The New

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:06.600
Yorker or Rolling Stone [Magazine] or any
kind of article that they’re interested

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:13.590
in making a movie of, you have to evaluate
it as a potential movie, so you’re asking

00:05:13.590 --> 00:05:18.720
yourself “Does it have the 3 or 4 act structure
of a commercial film?”

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:22.760
Film Courage: That interesting because it
sounds actually fairly intimidating because

00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:25.810
I know I’ve been in situations like this
where no one really tells you what the rules

00:05:25.810 --> 00:05:26.810
are.

00:05:26.810 --> 00:05:29.732
You kind of (as you said) intuit or watch
other people [their actions].

00:05:29.732 --> 00:05:30.750
So how was that?

00:05:30.750 --> 00:05:34.490
Were people actually giving you tips on how
to deal with all that?

00:05:34.490 --> 00:05:36.820
Or no, was it was every man for himself?

00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:37.920
Daniel Calvisi: Not a whole lot.

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:40.060
You didn’t get a whole lot of feedback.

00:05:40.060 --> 00:05:44.580
When I was first starting out, maybe a few
supervisors might have given me a few little

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:45.620
tips about coverage.

00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:48.740
But after awhile they don’t really give
you any feedback.

00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:54.780
You don’t even know where this script goes
after you send in your coverage report.

00:05:54.780 --> 00:06:00.570
So I would really have to ask afterward like
“Hey, that script that I really loved…did

00:06:00.570 --> 00:06:01.690
anything happen with it?”

00:06:01.690 --> 00:06:06.290
And so they would tell me “Oh, we didn’t
buy it but Fox bought it” or something like

00:06:06.290 --> 00:06:08.350
that.

00:06:08.350 --> 00:06:12.650
As a reader, you’re not told anything [laughs],
so you have to inquire.

00:06:12.650 --> 00:06:15.410
You have to have the initiative to see what
happens after that.

00:06:15.410 --> 00:06:20.990
And you’re not part of the process of development
or purchasing or bidding on it or anything

00:06:20.990 --> 00:06:22.270
like that.

00:06:22.270 --> 00:06:28.300
After you read it once, maybe twice, it’s
out of your hands and then your supervisors,

00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:34.380
the coordinators, the execs, the VPs, they’re
the ones who are going to be deciding whether

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:36.790
the company is going to option it or not.

00:06:36.790 --> 00:06:40.870
Film Courage: And that’s fascinating…I
wonder were there certain scripts that you

00:06:40.870 --> 00:06:45.961
would read just the first 10 pages of and
you just knew right away that this was not

00:06:45.961 --> 00:06:46.961
going to go anywhere?

00:06:46.961 --> 00:06:47.961
Daniel Calvisi: Yeah…

00:06:47.961 --> 00:06:50.410
Film Courage: So why?Daniel Calvisi: I mean
as crazy as it sounds, it could be the first

00:06:50.410 --> 00:06:51.410
paragraph.

00:06:51.410 --> 00:06:53.139
It could be the first line.

00:06:53.139 --> 00:06:57.430
I mean if there are two typos in the first
line…like…it’s probably not going to

00:06:57.430 --> 00:07:00.000
go anywhere, you know?

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:08.730
It’s very rare (incredibly rare) that the
first 10, 20, 30 pages will be weak and then

00:07:08.730 --> 00:07:12.010
the script gets a whole lot better later,
you know?

00:07:12.010 --> 00:07:18.720
If a writer doesn’t know how to write a
great Act 1, they’re not going to learn

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:21.894
later and suddenly turn it around and have
a great Act 2(a) or Act 2(b).

00:07:21.894 --> 00:07:22.894
Question for the Viewers: Are you friends
with anyone who is or was a Hollywood script

00:07:22.894 --> 00:07:23.110
reader?

